---

<Base title="7. Functions — C# vs TypeScript" description="7. Functions: side-by-side C# and TypeScript examples." slug="functions">
  <div slot="header" id="top">
    <p><small><a href="/">C# vs TS</a> / 7. Functions</small></p>
    <h1>7. Functions</h1>
    <p><strong>How to read:</strong> left is C#, middle is TypeScript, right is blank Mystery (yours). Narrow screens stack.</p>
  </div>

  <section id="functions">
  
  <h2>7. Functions</h2>
  <h3>7a. Declarations, arrows, function types</h3>
  <Compare>
    <div slot="left">
      <pre><code>int Add(int a, int b) =&gt; a + b;

int Fib(int n)
&#123;
  if (n &lt;= 1) return n;
  return Fib(n - 1) + Fib(n - 2);
&#125;

Func&lt;int, int, int&gt; add = (a, b) =&gt; a + b;
Func&lt;int&gt; getRandom = () =&gt; Random.Shared.Next();

// closures capture variables (by reference)
int factor = 10;
Func&lt;int, int&gt; scale = x =&gt; x * factor;</code></pre>
    </div>
    <div slot="right">
      <pre><code>function add(a: number, b: number): number &#123; return a + b; &#125;
const add2 = (a: number, b: number): number =&gt; a + b;

const fib = (n: number): number =&gt;
  n &lt;= 1 ? n : fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2);

type Add = (a: number, b: number) =&gt; number;
const add3: Add = (a, b) =&gt; a + b;

// closures capture by reference too
let factor = 10;
const scale = (x: number): number =&gt; x * factor;</code></pre>
    </div>
  </Compare>
  <h3>7b. Defaults, named args, params</h3>
  <Compare>
    <div slot="left">
      <pre><code>void Greet(string name = "world", bool loud = false)
&#123; /* ... */ &#125;
Greet(loud: true); // true named args
Greet("Ada");

int Sum(params int[] nums) =&gt; nums.Sum();
Sum(1, 2, 3);</code></pre>
    </div>
    <div slot="right">
      <pre><code>function greet(name = "world", loud = false) &#123; /* ... */ &#125;
greet(undefined, true); // placeholder for "named"
greet("Ada");

// idiomatic named args: options object + destructuring
function greetOpts(&#123; name = "world", loud = false &#125;: &#123; name?: string; loud?: boolean &#125;) &#123;&#125;
greetOpts(&#123; loud: true &#125;);

function sum(...nums: number[]): number &#123;
  return nums.reduce((a, b) =&gt; a + b, 0);
&#125;
sum(1, 2, 3); sum(...[1, 2]);</code></pre>
    </div>
  </Compare>
  <ul>
    <li>Arrow <code>=&gt;</code> exists in both. C# lambdas need a delegate type (<code>Func/Action</code>); TS function types are structural.</li>
    <li>C# has true named args. TS fakes them with a destructured options object — prefer that once you have &gt;2 params.</li>
    <li><code>this</code>: C# methods capture per instance. TS arrows capture outer <code>this</code>; regular <code>function</code> gets dynamic <code>this</code> — prefer arrows for callbacks. Use <code>this: void</code> param to forbid dynamic this.</li>
  </ul>

  </section>

